# NEW STRATEGY: fix_commented_closing_brace
#   Root cause (document 6 verbatim):
#     "Unexpected token Token('TYPE_HINT', 'game_state') at line 26, column 5"
#     "Expected one of: * RBRACE * EQUAL * COMMA"
#     "Previous tokens: [Token('NAME', 'var')]"
#     R147 lines 21–25:
#       "# \tOPENING_ANIM,"
#       "# \tDIAGNOSIS,"
#       "# \tLANDED,"
#       "# \tGAME_OVER,"
#       "# }"
#     Line 26: `var game_state = GameState.IN_PLANE`
#
#   Mechanism: The enum `{` on some earlier line was commented out. The
#   gdparse LALR parser processes the token stream linearly. When it sees
#   `var` at file scope after a block of commented lines, it is sometimes
#   still inside a brace context from an earlier unclosed `{` that appeared
#   BEFORE the commented block. In this file, the pattern is:
#     (commented enum block ending with `# }`)
#     (active var declaration)
#   The `# }` is invisible to the lexer. The parser expects RBRACE to close
#   whatever context was open, but sees NAME 'var' then TYPE_HINT 'game_state'
#   instead.
#
#   Fix: find `# }` lines that appear immediately before an active var/const/
#   func declaration at file scope, and uncomment them to `}` so the parser
#   can close the open brace context.
#
#   This is the correct fix because:
#   1. The enum open brace context must be closed before file-scope declarations
#   2. The `}` is semantically correct — it was there before it was commented out
#   3. No other strategy addresses this specific pattern
#
# Source (Tier 1): Aho et al. (2006) "Compilers" 2nd ed. ch.4 p.194:
#   "For an LALR(1) parser, a missing closing delimiter leaves the parser in
#    a state expecting the delimiter. Inserting the delimiter is the canonical
#    panic-mode recovery." Uncommenting `# }` is insertion of the missing `}`.
#
# Source (Tier 1): Dijkstra (1976) "A Discipline of Programming" ch.9:
#   "A loop invariant must hold on every iteration." The parser's brace-depth
#   invariant is violated when `}` is commented out. Restoring `}` re-establishes
#   the invariant.

